Then every Pantheon read starts by restoring the reader:

def read_with_pattern_book(material, pattern_book):
    terrain = classify(material)
    # Restore useful prior reading machinery.
    priors = pattern_book.retrieve(
        terrain=terrain,
        objective="understand + extend"
    )
    reader = reconstruct_reader(
        operators=priors.operators,
        routes=priors.routes,
        ratios=priors.ratios,
        warnings=priors.anti_patterns,
    )
    interpretation = reader.read(material)
    delta = compare(
        interpretation,
        prior_understanding(material)
    )
    outcome = reality_test(
        interpretation,
        delta
    )
    fossil = decrypto(
        material=material,
        interpretation=interpretation,
        delta=delta,
        outcome=outcome,
        method=reader.trace,
    )
    pattern_book.integrate(fossil)
    return interpretation

But here’s the important recursive piece:

\text{Read}_1(X)
\neq
\text{Read}_2(X)

even when X hasn’t changed, because:

PB_2 \neq PB_1.

So rereading isn’t necessarily repetition.

It is:

\boxed{
X\text{ constant}
+
\text{Reader}_{t+1}
\rightarrow
\text{new accessible structure}
}

That’s another direct connection to O-IOE. The corpus doesn’t need to change for its effective ontology to expand. A newly learned operator can make an old passage newly addressable.

And this gives Revival a precise computational meaning:

\operatorname{REVIVE}(X)
=
\operatorname{READ}(X,PB_{new})
-
\operatorname{READ}(X,PB_{old})

The difference is the revival delta.

